---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// Rewritten against the bioRxiv v2 PDF (posted 4 March 2025). The previous version summarised a
// print of the bioRxiv *web page* and quoted AUPRC point values (0.76, 0.74, 0.79, 0.78 …) that
// appear nowhere in the paper's text -- they live, if anywhere, in figure panels. This page states
// the comparative findings the paper makes in words, which is what it actually establishes.
// Checked by `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug traitgym`.
const title = "Benchmarking DNA Sequence Models for Causal Regulatory Variant Prediction in Human Genetics";
const description = "Technical summary of TraitGym (Benegas et al., UC Berkeley, bioRxiv 2025): a curated benchmark of causal regulatory variants across 113 Mendelian and 83 complex traits, with matched controls, comparing sequence-to-function models against DNA language models.";
const readingTime = "22 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Benchmarking & Variant Effect Prediction</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">bioRxiv preprint · v2, 4 March 2025 (not peer reviewed)</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Gonzalo Benegas, Carlos Albors, Alan J. Aw, Chengzhong Ye, Yun S. Song</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> University of California, Berkeley</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.11.637758" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1101/2025.02.11.637758</a> · bioRxiv, version 2</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      Two families of model claim to predict which non-coding variants matter: <strong>supervised
      sequence-to-function models</strong> trained on functional genomics, and <strong>self-supervised DNA
      language models</strong>. Comparing them has been impossible because the field lacked consistently
      curated labels. <strong>TraitGym</strong> supplies them — causal or strong-candidate regulatory variants
      across <strong>113 Mendelian and 83 complex traits</strong>, each paired with
      <strong>nine carefully matched controls</strong> — and the answer is not a single winner:
      <strong>CADD and GPN-MSA lead on Mendelian and complex <em>disease</em> traits, while Enformer and
      Borzoi lead on complex <em>non-disease</em> traits</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. Why the Field Needed This Benchmark</a></li>
        <li><a href="#curation">2. Dataset Curation: Where the Positives Come From</a></li>
        <li><a href="#matching">3. The Control Matching That Makes It Hard</a></li>
        <li><a href="#evaluation">4. Evaluation: Leave-One-Chromosome-Out AUPRC</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">5. Results: No Single Winner</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ensembles">6. Ensembles, and Where They Stop Helping</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & How to Read the Rankings</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. Why the Field Needed This Benchmark</h2>
      <p>
        The paper's diagnosis is that the field <strong>lacks consistently curated datasets with accurate
        labels, especially for non-coding variants</strong>. Without them, every model reports on its own
        evaluation set, and the comparisons that matter — supervised versus self-supervised, alignment-based
        versus sequence-only — cannot be made.
      </p>
      <p>
        TraitGym frames causal variant prediction as <strong>binary classification</strong> and benchmarks four
        families: functional-genomics-supervised models, self-supervised DNA language models, models combining
        ML predictions with curated annotation features, and ensembles of these.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="curation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. Dataset Curation: Where the Positives Come From</h2>

      <h3>Mendelian traits</h3>
      <p>
        Curated causal non-coding variants for <strong>113 Mendelian diseases</strong> were collected from
        <strong>OMIM</strong>. For additional stringency, variants with <strong>minor allele frequency above
        0.1% in gnomAD</strong> were filtered out — a variant that common is unlikely to be the cause of a
        rare Mendelian disease, so its presence in the positive set would be a labelling error.
      </p>

      <h3>Complex traits</h3>
      <p>
        For <strong>83 complex traits</strong>, positives are UK Biobank variants with statistical fine-mapping
        <strong>posterior inclusion probability (PIP) above 0.9</strong>, and controls are variants with
        <strong>PIP below 0.01</strong>. These are "strong candidates" rather than known causal variants, and
        the paper says so — the label quality differs in kind between the two halves of the benchmark.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> Two datasets, two epistemic statuses
        </div>
        <p>
          The Mendelian positives are curated causal variants; the complex-trait positives are fine-mapping
          output. A model that does better on one is not necessarily better at "finding causal variants" — it
          may be better at whatever produced that label set. Keeping the two separate throughout the results
          is what lets the paper say something specific rather than something averaged.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="matching" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. The Control Matching That Makes It Hard</h2>
      <p>
        The benchmark's difficulty is set by its negatives. TraitGym uses <strong>nine matched control
        variants for each putatively causal variant, matched within each chromosome</strong>, on the features
        that would otherwise let a model win without learning anything:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Dataset</th>
              <th>Positives</th>
              <th>Controls matched on</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Mendelian</strong></td>
              <td>OMIM curated causal, MAF ≤ 0.1% in gnomAD</td>
              <td>Consequence class, TSS distance</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Complex traits</strong></td>
              <td>UK Biobank PIP &gt; 0.9</td>
              <td>MAF, LD score, TSS distance (controls at PIP &lt; 0.01)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Matching on TSS distance is the crucial one. Regulatory variants cluster near transcription start
        sites, so an unmatched benchmark can be won by a model that has learned only "close to a TSS" —
        which is a fact about genome organisation, not about the variant. Matching on consequence class and
        LD score removes the analogous shortcuts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper contrasts this with prior work using a <strong>much larger, non-subsampled negative set of
        2.6 million variants</strong>, which is more realistic in prevalence but makes evaluating the latest,
        computationally expensive models impractical. Nine-to-one is a deliberate compromise between realism
        and being able to run Evo2 at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="evaluation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Evaluation: Leave-One-Chromosome-Out AUPRC</h2>
      <p>
        The metric is <strong>area under the precision–recall curve, computed per chromosome</strong>, for a
        model trained on the remaining chromosomes, then combined as a <strong>weighted average across
        chromosomes based on sample size, with a standard error estimate</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three choices are doing work here. <strong>AUPRC rather than AUROC</strong>, because the classes are
        imbalanced nine to one and AUROC is optimistic under imbalance. <strong>Per chromosome</strong>,
        because variants on the same chromosome share LD and locus-level features, so a random split would
        leak. And <strong>a standard error</strong>, because without one a ranking of close models is not
        interpretable — which is precisely why this page does not quote point estimates lifted from a figure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="results" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. Results: No Single Winner</h2>

      <h3>The headline split</h3>
      <p>
        The paper's central finding is a division of labour, stated plainly in its abstract:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model family</th>
              <th>Representatives</th>
              <th>Where it compares favorably</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Alignment-based</strong></td>
              <td>CADD, GPN-MSA</td>
              <td><strong>Mendelian traits and complex <em>disease</em> traits</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Functional-genomics-supervised</strong></td>
              <td>Enformer, Borzoi</td>
              <td><strong>Complex <em>non-disease</em> traits</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Self-supervised DNA LM</strong></td>
              <td>Evo2</td>
              <td>Gains substantially with scale, but <strong>still lags alignment-based models</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Where Evo2 struggles</h3>
      <p>
        Evo2 <strong>struggles particularly with enhancer variants</strong>. The paper's explanation is
        structural rather than incidental: a genome-wide self-supervised objective does not
        <strong>give a special weight to enhancers, which are sparsely represented in genomes such as that of
        humans</strong>. A model trained to predict the next base spends its capacity in proportion to how
        much sequence there is, and enhancers are a small fraction of it.
      </p>

      <h3>The stratifications</h3>
      <p>
        Beyond the headline, results are broken out by disease versus non-disease complex traits, by
        pleiotropic versus non-pleiotropic variants, and by whether a variant overlaps a fine-mapped eQTL.
        The last is the sharpest test of the supervised family: eQTL overlap is where functional-genomics
        training data is most directly relevant.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ensembles" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Ensembles, and Where They Stop Helping</h2>
      <p>
        On the <strong>complex traits</strong> dataset, ensembling the three models achieves the best
        performance, with a <strong>particularly high jump when combining Borzoi with either CADD or
        GPN-MSA</strong>. That is the expected shape when two families carry genuinely different information —
        cellular molecular activity from one, organismal purifying selection from the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <strong>Mendelian</strong> dataset the result reverses: <strong>ensembling the full features
        from different models does not improve upon CADD input features</strong>. Where the signal is
        essentially conservation, adding functional-genomics predictions contributes nothing that CADD's own
        feature set does not already carry.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The transferable lesson
        </div>
        <p>
          "Which variant-effect predictor is best?" is the wrong question, and this benchmark is constructed
          to show why. The answer depends on trait class, and the mechanism is legible: Mendelian disease
          variants are under strong purifying selection and so are visible to alignments; complex non-disease
          traits are quantitative and tissue-specific, which is what functional-genomics models measure.
          Choose the predictor that matches the selection regime of the trait.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation</h2>

      <h3>The scoring interface</h3>
      <p>
        Every model is reduced to one number per variant. Each consumes the reference and alternate allele
        together with the surrounding DNA sequence context, and emits a score:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="s(v) = f\big(x_{\text{ref}},\, x_{\text{alt}},\, \text{context}\big) \in \mathbb{R}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Collapsing every model to this interface is what makes the comparison possible at all, and it is also
        the benchmark's main abstraction cost: a model producing per-tissue tracks must choose an aggregation
        before it can be scored.
      </p>

      <h3>The aggregated metric</h3>
      <p>
        With <Katex tex="n_c" /> evaluation variants on chromosome <Katex tex="c" />:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\overline{\text{AUPRC}} = \frac{\sum_{c} n_c \cdot \text{AUPRC}_c}{\sum_{c} n_c}, \qquad \text{AUPRC}_c \text{ evaluated on } c \text{, trained on } \{1..22\}\setminus c" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Note the baseline: with nine controls per positive, a random classifier achieves AUPRC ≈ 0.1, so
        absolute values should be read against that floor rather than against 0.5.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & How to Read the Rankings</h2>

      <h3>Label provenance</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Complex-trait positives are fine-mapping output, not established causal variants.</strong> A model correlated with whatever fine-mapping used — LD, MAF, functional priors — is advantaged in a way that does not reflect causal-variant detection.</li>
        <li><strong>OMIM curation reflects historical attention.</strong> Well-studied disease genes are over-represented, so Mendelian performance is partly a statement about coverage of known biology.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Benchmark construction</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nine-to-one is not the real prevalence.</strong> Genome-wide, causal variants are far rarer, and precision at realistic prevalence will be much lower than any AUPRC here implies. The paper is explicit that the subsampling exists to make expensive models evaluable.</li>
        <li><strong>Matching is a modelling choice.</strong> Controls matched on TSS distance and consequence remove shortcuts, but a genuinely causal signal correlated with those features is removed along with them — matching can only be as good as the confounders anticipated.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Status of the work</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>This is a preprint, version 2, not peer reviewed</strong>, and the model landscape it ranks moves quickly. Read the family-level conclusions, which are mechanistic, ahead of the ordering of any two specific models.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Source note
        </div>
        <p>
          Written against the bioRxiv v2 PDF. An earlier version of this page was built from a print of the
          bioRxiv web page rather than the paper, and quoted AUPRC point values that do not appear in the
          text; the comparative findings above are the ones the paper states in words.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
